@*
    The observer stream, ported — not RoundLog's panel. The console keeps a log because it
    clears the screen between turns for concealment; a browser never loses its scrollback
    (§3.11).

    ⚠️ aria-live="polite", never assertive: a bot table says something every second or so, and
    an assertive region would interrupt a screen reader continuously (§3.11 B8, WCAG 4.1.3).
    The hand and the seats are NOT live regions; only this is.

    🔥 THE TRAP, AND HOW IT IS ANSWERED (BUILD-PLAN P13.5). P13.5 demoted the log from the main
    channel to a panel you open — and a hidden live region announces nothing. Putting this list
    behind display:none, or inside a closed <details>, would take the whole narration away from
    exactly the people who were relying on it most, in the packet that increased the reliance.

    So the list is ALWAYS RENDERED AND ALWAYS LIVE. Closing the panel clips it to a pixel with
    the same recipe .said uses — off screen, still laid out, still in the accessibility tree,
    still announcing every line as it arrives. What closing really changes is three things a
    sighted mouse-and-keyboard user cares about and a screen reader does not: the panel's
    height, whether the scroller is in the tab order, and whether the last line is repeated as a
    one-line ticker for the eye.

    ⚠️ The ticker is aria-hidden. It is the newest line drawn twice, and a screen reader that
    read both would hear every move of the game twice over.
*@

<section class="log @(_open ? "open" : "shut")" aria-labelledby="log-heading">
    <h2 id="log-heading">
        <button type="button"
                class="toggle"
                aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
                aria-controls="log-lines"
                @onclick="Toggle">
            @*
                ⚠️ No hidden twin, and no aria-label. aria-expanded already says open or closed,
                and a .said repeating it made the button announce itself as "Round log, open the
                round log, 1 lines" — the same thing three times. This is the plain disclosure
                pattern, and the plain one is the one that reads correctly.
            *@
            <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">@(_open ? "▾" : "▸")</span>
            <span class="what">Round log</span>
            <span class="count">@Lines.Count</span>
        </button>
    </h2>

    @*
        The newest line, for the eye only, while the panel is closed. The felt says what is
        happening; this says what just happened, in one line, without opening anything.
    *@
    @if (!_open && Lines.Count > 0)
    {
        @*
            ⚠️ The cards come with it. Half of these lines end in a colon — "Aung Aung discarded"
            — and the card is the half that says what happened. aria-hidden covers the chips
            inside too, so their .said text is not read twice.
        *@
        <p class="latest @Tone(Lines[^1])" aria-hidden="true">
            @Lines[^1].Text
            @foreach (var card in Lines[^1].Cards)
            {
                <CardChip @key="card.Id.Value" Card="card" />
            }
        </p>
    }

    @*
        ⚠️ The scroller is a column-reverse flex box wrapping a normally-ordered list, which is
        the one way to keep a growing log pinned to its latest line without JavaScript. The DOM
        order stays oldest-first — which is the reading order, and what a screen reader is
        given — while the box's scroll origin is the bottom, so the newest line is the one on
        screen. A log that showed its oldest line for ever would be a log nobody watches.
    *@
    @*
        Focusable and named while it is open: a scrolling region has to be reachable by keyboard
        or its older lines are unreachable without a pointer (WCAG 2.1.1, §3.11 B6). Closed, it
        is taken out of the tab order — a focus stop on a box nobody can see is a keyboard trap
        in everything but name — while staying in the accessibility tree, which is the half that
        matters (see the note above).
    *@
    <div id="log-lines"
         class="scroller"
         tabindex="@(_open ? 0 : -1)"
         aria-label="Round log — scroll for earlier lines">
        <ol aria-live="polite" aria-relevant="additions" aria-atomic="false">
            @foreach (var line in Lines)
            {
                <li @key="line.Sequence" class="line @Tone(line)">
                    <span class="round" aria-hidden="true">@line.Round</span>
                    <span class="text">
                        @line.Text
                        @foreach (var card in line.Cards)
                        {
                            <CardChip @key="card.Id.Value" Card="card" />
                        }
                    </span>
                </li>
            }
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>Everything said so far, oldest first.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public IReadOnlyList<LogLine> Lines { get; set; } = [];

    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing calls <c>StateHasChanged</c> here — <c>ComponentBase</c> already re-renders
    /// after an event handler of its own (§3.11 C15).
    /// </remarks>
    private void Toggle() => _open = !_open;

    /// <summary>
    /// A class name per tone. <b>Nothing here is understood by colour</b> — every line is a
    /// whole sentence, and the tone only tints it (§3.11 A2).
    /// </summary>
    private static string Tone(LogLine line) => line.Tone switch
    {
        LogTone.Money => "money",
        LogTone.Good => "good",
        LogTone.Quiet => "quiet",
        LogTone.Bad => "bad",
        _ => "normal"
    };
}
